

Essential Terraform Blocks

• terraform (Root Configuration): Sets ground rules for the project,
	including the required Terraform CLI version and provider version
	constraints inside a required_providers nested block. Visit the
	HashiCorp Terraform Block Reference to view syntax rules for version
	pinning and cloud settings.
• provider: Connects Terraform to your target cloud or SaaS platform
	(such as AWS, Azure, or GCP) and configures authentication, regions,
	or endpoint defaults.
• resource: Declares actual infrastructure components (such as an
	EC2 instance, VPC, or database) using a unique combination of
	resource type and resource name.
• data: Queries and fetches existing, read-only infrastructure or
	external data from your cloud provider so you can reference it
	inside your configuration.
• variable: Defines input parameters for your modules or root
	configuration, allowing you to pass customizable values across
	different environments.
• output: Exposes specific values (such as an IP address, DNS name,
	or ID) from your root or child module so other modules or operators
	can read them after an apply.
• locals: Defines local (private) variables or intermediate expressions
	to simplify complex calculations and prevent code duplication within
	a module.o
• module: Calls and reuses a child module from a local path, git
	repository, or the public registry to compose larger infrastructure
	architectures.
• backend: Configures where and how your state file is stored
	remotely (such as Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, or Google Cloud
	Storage) to enable team collaboration and state locking.
• dynamic: Programmatically generates repeating nested configuration
	blocks (such as security group ingress rules or IAM policies) from
	lists or maps to keep your code DRY.
• moved: Use to indicate to terraform that a resource has moved, preventing
	terraform from destroying and recreating the resource.
